[03:29:49] section='''# 13. Resolution Tree
[03:29:49] **Status: DECIDED (AR-002, Class C).** The contract below is target intent,
[03:29:49] not a claim that detail endpoints or semantic resolution traces exist today.
[03:29:49] [ADR-002](architecture/decisions/ADR-002-resolution-tree-http-contract.md)
[03:29:49] records the alternatives and rationale. This section owns the wire contract.
[03:29:49] ## 13.1 Detail operations and explicit inputs
[03:29:49] Add "'`POST /api/crafting/profit/resolution` and
[03:29:49] `POST /api/crafting/discovery/resolution`. These paths are fixed by this decision,
[03:29:49] unlike the illustrative endpoints in section 9. Both are read-only calculations;
[03:29:49] neither consumes persisted inventory nor triggers GW2 synchronization.
[03:29:49] Each required JSON body contains `recipeId` (positive integer) and `calculation`
[03:29:49] (object). `calculation` uses the corresponding existing table request contract:
[03:29:49] - Profit: `scope` (`kind`: `ALL`, `DISCIPLINE`, `CHARACTER_DISCIPLINE`, plus
[03:29:49]   `discipline`, `characterName`, `rating` as applicable) and `settings`.
[03:29:49] - Discovery: `scope` (`discipline`, `characterName`, `rating`), independent nullable
[03:29:49]   `inventoryCharacterName`, and `settings`. Preserve the separate inventory
[03:29:49]   character and the existing null/unfiltered-pool semantics.
[03:29:49] - Settings retain `useOwnMats`, `allowBuying`, `maxBuyCopper`, `listingSell`,
[03:29:49]   `listingBuy`, and Profit'"'s "'`dailyBuyInsteadOfCraft`. Discovery fixes the latter
[03:29:49]   to false and does not accept it as a selectable input. Existing validation,
[03:29:49]   defaults and rating semantics apply; this adds no new calculation controls.
[03:29:49] The browser copies the table response'"'s effective inputs into the appropriate
[03:29:49] request fields rather than relying on defaults again. It must not send row
[03:29:49] numbers, prices, material maps or a prior service/context identifier as inputs.
[03:29:49] Recipe identity is the recipe ID, never merely its output item ID.
[03:29:49] ## 13.2 Calculation lifetime and consistency
[03:29:49] Validate first, then create one fresh application-service calculation context
[03:29:49] for the detail operation. Load data, check the recipe against the newly returned
[03:29:49] visible candidate set, evaluate and produce its explanation inside that operation.
[03:29:49] Initially reusing the existing reload use case is acceptable; selected-recipe
[03:29:49] optimization must preserve its eligibility and domain semantics. Never look up a
[03:29:49] previous request's service or share mutable last-result fields across requests.
[03:29:49] An empty Discovery reload cannot expose any previous result or lookup state.
[03:29:49] Capture the loaded inputs used by this operation and use them for both its row
[03:29:49] summary and tree evaluation. Do not reload prices, inventory or graph midway
[03:29:49] through explanation construction. Release request-local state after completion or
[03:29:49] failure; no retained calculation session, TTL, cache token, durable snapshot or
[03:29:49] new task store is required. Shared reference data must not carry mutable
[03:29:49] simulation/inventory state between operations.
[03:29:49] This is a **fresh calculation**, not retrieval of the table's historical result.
[03:29:49] Even identical input values may yield different results after synchronization or
[03:29:49] price changes. There is no cross-request snapshot guarantee and no claim of an
[03:29:49] atomic database-plus-graph snapshot during loading. Request-local reuse prevents
[03:29:49] explanation construction from introducing a second data read, but does not settle
[03:29:50] future synchronization consistency design (section 23 / UD-007).
[03:29:50] ## 13.3 Successful response
[03:29:50] The completed response is JSON with these required fields:
[03:29:50] | Field | Type and meaning |
[03:29:50] | --- | --- |
[03:29:50] | "'`recipeId` | Requested recipe ID. |
[03:29:50] | `calculation` | Effective scope and settings, shaped as the corresponding table response'"'s scope/settings; Discovery also includes nullable "'`inventoryCharacterName`. All effective settings are explicit, including Discovery'"'s fixed daily setting. |
[03:29:50] | "'`consistency` | Literal `FRESH_CALCULATION`. |
[03:29:50] | `calculatedAt` | UTC ISO-8601 completion timestamp; informational, not a data version or snapshot identifier. |
[03:29:50] | `row` | Existing shared crafting row DTO for this recipe, recalculated in this operation; retains its existing per-craft/total field meanings and nullability. |
[03:29:50] | `treeStatus` | `AVAILABLE` or `RESULT_UNAVAILABLE`. |
[03:29:50] | `treeBasis` | Literal `SINGLE_CRAFT`: one execution of the selected recipe from this operation'"'s initial inventory, budget and daily state, applying the selected settings and domain rules. |
[03:29:50] | "'`tree` | Recursive node below, or null only when `treeStatus` is `RESULT_UNAVAILABLE`. |
[03:29:50] The single-craft explanation is a domain evaluation, not multiplication of a
[03:29:50] recipe skeleton and not a trace of all `row.craftableCount` crafts or the next
[03:29:50] craft after exhausting a simulation. Its root requests the recipe'"'s output count.
[03:29:50] The row remains the normal simulation summary. In particular, tree costs must
[03:29:50] not be presented as the row's multi-craft totals; the browser labels the basis.
[03:29:50] Both evaluations use the same captured inputs and authoritative resolver rules.
[03:29:50] A blocked first craft still returns an available explanation with reasons. If the
[03:29:50] backend has no calculation result, retain the row's existing unavailable/null
[03:29:50] semantics and return "'`RESULT_UNAVAILABLE`, not a fabricated empty tree.
[03:29:50] Each recursive requirement node has exactly this initial semantic shape:
[03:29:50] | Field | Type and meaning |
[03:29:50] | --- | --- |
[03:29:50] | `itemId` | Positive integer item identifier. |
[03:29:50] | `itemName` | String or null when metadata is unavailable; display may fall back to the ID. |
[03:29:50] | `requestedQuantity` | Nonnegative integer units required by this occurrence. |
[03:29:50] | `inventoryQuantity`, `craftedQuantity`, `boughtQuantity`, `missingQuantity` | Nonnegative integer contributions to that requirement, decided by the backend. Crafted quantity means units used here, not surplus output. Their sum equals requested quantity. |
[03:29:50] | `recipeId` | Selected producing recipe ID, or null when no crafting path is selected. Root identifies the requested recipe. |
[03:29:50] | `craftCount`, `producedQuantity` | Nonnegative integers for the selected crafting contribution; zero when none completes. Production can exceed crafted quantity because of recipe batch size. |
[03:29:50] | `characterName` | Assigned crafting character or null when not applicable/not assigned; preserve backend character selection. |
[03:29:50] | `methods` | Array containing the actually used contributions from `INVENTORY`, `CRAFT`, `BUY`; multiple methods are allowed for split sourcing. Empty if no contribution succeeds. |
[03:29:50] | `states` | Array of backend flags from `BLOCKED`, `PRICE_UNAVAILABLE`, `DAILY_LIMIT`, `UNVALUED_NONTRADEABLE`; empty if none apply. These may coexist with methods. |
[03:29:50] | `blockedReasons` | Array of the explicit reasons in DOMAIN_SPEC section 42 (`NO_RECIPE`, `BUYING_DISABLED`, `DAILY_LIMIT`, `CYCLE_DETECTED`, `PRICE_UNAVAILABLE`, `RECIPE_NOT_ALLOWED`, `INSUFFICIENT_BUDGET`); empty for an unblocked node. |
[03:29:50] | `cashCostCopper`, `opportunityCostCopper`, `effectiveCostCopper` | Backend-provided numeric amounts in copper for this node'"'s complete requirement, inclusive of descendants; null when a complete value cannot be established. Preserve domain precision; no UI rounding in transport. Known zero is distinct from null. |
[03:29:50] | "'`children` | Ordered array of ingredient requirement nodes for the selected crafting path (including a blocked attempted path); empty for leaves. |
[03:29:50] Costs on ancestors include descendant costs; the frontend must not add them again.
[03:29:50] For a blocked requirement, a complete cost is null rather than a misleading
[03:29:50] partial total. A domain-established zero for an unvalued non-tradable item remains
[03:29:50] zero with its explicit state. Unknown purchase prices never become zero. Failed
[03:29:50] speculative paths must not leak committed quantities or costs. Cycle detection
[03:29:50] terminates with a finite blocked node; no object references or graph back-links
[03:29:50] are serialized. Repeated items in different branches remain separate occurrences;
[03:29:50] a presentation key may use the child-index path and is not a persistent node ID.
[03:29:50] Do not truncate a tree silently or infer states from raw quotes in a mapper.
[03:29:50] The domain produces resolution choices, quantities, valuation and failure facts;
[03:29:50] the application coordinates the captured context, and the API maps those facts
[03:29:50] into DTOs. Existing `Node.action` text and a first-recipe dependency expansion are
[03:29:51] not sufficient evidence of actual resolution. The implementation must obtain a
[03:29:51] semantic trace from authoritative resolution, not build a competing resolver in
[03:29:51] the controller, DTO mapper or browser. Domain types remain independent of JSON,
[03:29:51] HTTP and JavaFX. No color, widget, CSS class or expansion state belongs in this
[03:29:51] schema. Unknown future state/reason codes must remain visibly representable,
[03:29:51] never silently treated as success.
[03:29:51] ## 13.4 Missing results, errors and browser association
[03:29:51] A valid recipe ID absent from the fresh visible candidate set returns HTTP 404
[03:29:51] with the existing error-body shape `{ "error": "RECIPE_NOT_IN_CALCULATION",
[03:29:51] "message": "..." }`. This includes a recipe that is no longer discoverable;
[03:29:51] it does not assert that the recipe is absent from the global database. Invalid
[03:29:51] input uses existing 400 validation/malformed-request errors; store failures use
[03:29:51] 503 and unexpected calculation failures use 500 with existing safe error codes.
[03:29:51] Blocked domain paths and available rows with unavailable calculation results are
[03:29:51] completed HTTP 200 responses, not transport failures. Existing table endpoints
[03:29:51] retain their empty-list 200 behavior and do not eagerly attach trees.
[03:29:51] The browser associates a request with feature, recipe ID, effective calculation
[03:29:51] inputs and a local request generation. Changing selection or calculation inputs,
[03:29:51] starting a table reload, or leaving the view invalidates the previous detail.
[03:29:51] Cancellation is optional; ignoring late responses for invalid generations is
[03:29:51] required even for A-to-B-to-A selection. Accept a response only for the active
[03:29:51] generation and matching echoed identity/inputs. Sorting or formatting alone does
[03:29:51] not change calculation identity. Preserve valid user controls during refresh.
[03:29:51] Display the returned row and tree together as freshly calculated detail, with
[03:29:51] its single-craft basis. Do not label it as the exact explanation of the earlier
[03:29:51] table row or silently overwrite the old table row with it. Errors/unavailable
[03:29:51] results clear the active tree; they must not leave an old tree under a new
[03:29:51] selection. Expansion and rendering remain frontend concerns.
[03:29:51] ## 13.5 Migration, execution and verification constraints
[03:29:51] JavaFX continues to call application services in process. Keep the table routes
[03:29:51] and their response shapes compatible; adding detail does not force JavaFX through
[03:29:51] HTTP or attach tree-building work to every table row. Local class names and trace
[03:29:51] representation are implementation choices within these boundaries.
[03:29:51] The schema above defines the completed response. Per section 23 and UD-007,
[03:29:51] measure the detail operation on representative real data before fixing its
[03:29:51] synchronous/asynchronous execution policy: prefer direct HTTP 200 completion when
[03:29:51] consistently short; if measurements require a task, retain these inputs and
[03:29:51] completed payload and document the task transport before shipping the consumer.
[03:29:51] This decision does not prescribe or assume a new task mechanism.
[03:29:51] Phase 5 must still verify section 33'"'s complete browser-page timing, including
[03:29:51] any detail automatically loaded on navigation. Lazy selection does not excuse
[03:29:51] hiding required initial work or omitting results. No performance result is
[03:29:51] claimed by this contract. Verification must cover semantic split sourcing,
[03:29:51] blocked/unvalued nodes, null costs, cycles, fresh-input row/tree consistency,
[03:29:51] concurrent scope isolation, empty Discovery after populated calculation, and
[03:29:51] late browser responses. Test responsibilities remain owned by TEST_STRATEGY.md;
[03:29:51] the planner owns bounded implementation and verification work.
[03:29:51] ---
[03:29:51] '''
[03:29:51] p.write_text(s[:start]+section+s[end:],encoding='utf-8')
[03:29:51] adr=Path('docs/architecture/decisions/ADR-002-resolution-tree-http-contract.md')
[03:29:51] assert not adr.exists()
[03:29:51] adr.write_text('''# ADR-002 — Fresh resolution detail with explicit calculation inputs
[03:29:51] ## Status
[03:29:51] ACCEPTED — 2026-09-25. Class C: architecturally significant technical decision.
[03:29:51] Requires Product Owner decision: NO.
[03:29:51] ## Context
[03:29:51] AR-002 asks for the tree transport and calculation lifetime needed by Phase 5.
[03:29:51] CURRENT_ARCHITECTURE sections 5.5/5.6 establish request-local services and table
[03:29:51] rows without trees. Discovery's empty reload can retain instance lookup state.
[03:29:51] STORY-WEB-001's Result confirms the browser table has no tree contract.
[03:29:51] DOMAIN_SPEC sections 42–44 require meaningful failure states and explanations.
[03:29:51] ## Constraints
[03:29:51] Backend/domain authority, JavaFX coexistence, one configured account, incremental
[03:29:51] migration, no speculative infrastructure, and the complete-page performance gate
[03:29:51] remain binding. UD-007 requires measured execution-policy decisions. Historical
[03:29:51] snapshot retrieval is not an established requirement in the dispatched question.
[03:29:51] ## Decision
[03:29:51] Use independently calculated detail from explicit feature-specific inputs and
[03:29:51] recipe identity. TARGET_ARCHITECTURE section 13 owns the exact contract, context
[03:29:51] lifetime, single-craft explanation basis, browser association and failure rules.
[03:29:51] Do not introduce retained calculation sessions. The returned explanation and
[03:29:51] fresh summary use the operation's captured data, with an explicit distinction
[03:29:51] between the single-craft tree and the simulation summary.
[03:29:51] ## Rationale
[03:29:51] **Architectural judgment:** fresh detail is the simplest fit for this small,
[03:29:51] single-user migration. It extends the existing request isolation and avoids
[03:29:51] session eviction, memory quotas, restart recovery, expiry responses and coupling
[03:29:51] between a table request and later selection. Explicit effective inputs and local
[03:29:51] browser generations make association testable by bounded implementation changes.
[03:29:51] It adds no dependency, service or operational owner. The cost is repeated loading
[03:29:51] and computation, and no promise of reproducing an earlier table result.
[03:29:51] A single-craft explanation keeps the existing selected-recipe explanation scale
[03:29:51] without transmitting a potentially large trace of every simulated craft. It must
[03:29:51] be labelled precisely and produced by domain resolution; it cannot be treated as
[03:29:51] the explanation of aggregate simulation costs. This is transport/explanation
[03:29:51] structure, not a change to simulation counts, eligibility or economic rules.
[03:29:51] Inspection of craft.RecipeTreeBuilder shows dependency expansion using the first
[03:29:51] producing recipe and string actions, without inventory/buy/cost resolution.
[03:29:51] Therefore serializing the current Node is insufficient. This observed limitation
[03:29:51] requires planner-owned follow-up; this ADR neither fixes it nor claims a semantic
[03:29:51] trace already exists.
[03:29:51] ## Alternatives Considered
[03:29:51] - **Retained calculation ID/context (runner-up):** supports exact prior-table
[03:29:51]   association if all required inputs and results are retained immutably. Useful
[03:29:51]   when historical consistency is required, but adds expiry, ownership, cleanup,
[03:29:51]   concurrency and restart semantics without that requirement here. Rejected on
[03:29:51]   complexity, not an unmeasured performance claim.
[03:29:51] - **Embed every tree in table responses:** gives one response lifecycle but
[03:29:51]   forces unselected tree work and larger transport into full-page loading. No
[03:29:51]   evidence justifies this against the existing lazy-detail design and timing gate.
[03:29:51] - **Frontend reconstruction / raw dependency skeleton:** simpler to serialize,
[03:29:51]   but cannot satisfy backend authority for actual sourcing and special states.
[03:29:51] ## Consequences
[03:29:51] Fresh detail can differ from the table; its provenance and basis must be visible.
[03:29:51] Repeated work requires real measurements, not a speculative cache or task design.
[03:29:51] If exact historical retrieval becomes required, a later decision must establish
[03:29:51] retention and snapshot semantics; that would change the lifecycle but can reuse
[03:29:51] the semantic tree payload. No new library is selected, so release cadence and
[03:29:51] vendor support introduce no additional maintenance obligation in this decision.
[03:29:51] The planner owns domain trace/application/API/browser implementation and targeted
[03:29:51] verification. Current architecture remains unchanged by this documentation-only
[03:29:51] run. The existing dependency-only tree limitation and future synchronization
[03:29:51] snapshot consistency must not be disguised as guarantees of this contract.
[03:29:51] ## References
[03:29:51] - [Target architecture](../../TARGET_ARCHITECTURE.md), sections 12–14, 23, 33.
[03:29:51] - [Current architecture](../../CURRENT_ARCHITECTURE.md), sections 5.5, 5.6, 5.11.
[03:29:51] - [Domain specification](../../DOMAIN_SPEC.md), sections 42–44 and invariants.
[03:29:51] - [Roadmap](../../ROADMAP.md), Phase 5.
[03:29:51] - [UD-007](../../../agent/user-decisions/UD-007-http-long-running-sync-approach.md).
[03:29:51] - [AR-002](../../../agent/architect-requests/AR-002-resolution-tree-http-contract.md).
[03:29:51] - Source inspection: craft.Node, craft.RecipeTreeBuilder, application crafting
[03:29:51]   services and web.dto crafting requests/responses. No runtime measurements made.
