[20:06:57] p=\"docs/architecture/decisions/ADR-005-persistent-web-icon-cache.md\"
[20:06:57] assert not Path(p).exists()
[20:06:57] write(p,\"# ADR-005 — Persistent application-owned item icon cache\\n\\n## Status\\n\\nACCEPTED — 2026-09-25. Class C, architecturally significant technical decision.\\nSupersedes [ADR-004](ADR-004-web-item-icon-delivery.md).\\nRequires Product Owner Decision: NO.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\nRequest-005 explicitly changes the earlier requirement: a cold browser must be\\nable to reuse images the application already fetched, without contacting\\nArenaNet. The Product Owner authorizes persistent filesystem storage and\\non-demand misses, while rejecting unnecessary binary database storage,\\ndistributed caches and new services. This is sufficient authority to decide\\nthe technical design; no performance bottleneck is asserted.\\n\\nObserved through bounded source inspection: "'`IconSync` retains upstream URLs'"\\nin "'`items.icon_url`, backfills only null URLs on existing rows, writes'"\\n"'`items/{itemId}.png` under its supplied cache root, and records `icon_path`.'"\\nIt skips nonempty files and does not record which source version produced them.\\n"'`AppConfig` already exposes `ICON_CACHE_DIR` with a user-home default.'"\\nConsequently the root and desktop path capability are reusable, but old\\nfilenames alone cannot establish version-correct image content. No real cache\\ncontents, metadata coverage or runtime performance were audited.\\n\\n## Constraints\\n\\nTARGET_ARCHITECTURE section 12.1 owns the complete replacement contract.\\nBackend metadata acquisition and domain isolation remain binding. ROADMAP\\nPhase 5 requires JavaFX coexistence and the existing full-page performance\\ngate. API-009 and WEB-010 contain blocked old contracts and require a subsequent\\nplanner pass. No source, story, roadmap or deployment implementation is\\nauthorized by this architectural decision.\\n\\n## Decision\\n\\nSelect a shared, source-versioned filesystem cache behind an application image\\nendpoint, with normal browser HTTP caching. Reuse the desktop configuration\\nroot and converge future desktop/web binary acquisition on one adapter.\\nTARGET_ARCHITECTURE section 12.1 is the sole normative owner of route, key,\\nstorage, migration, HTTP, failure and verification details.\\n\\n## Rationale\\n\\nArchitectural judgment: filesystem reuse is the strongest fit for this\\nsingle-maintainer migration. It meets the now-explicit persistent-reuse\\nrequirement without another runtime, service, dependency ecosystem or binary\\ndatabase workload. The HTTP boundary keeps table/tree metadata enrichment and\\nimage delivery outside domain calculations. A shared adapter and nullable URL\\nfield are bounded responsibilities for coding agents and future item views.\\n\\nSource-versioned filenames avoid serving changed metadata through a stale\\nitem-ID browser URL, let multiple items share a binary, and avoid overwriting\\nfiles still used by old pages. They cost a small migration boundary and retain\\nold versions. Conservative legacy adoption avoids inventing historical source\\nprovenance. Desktop compatibility requires changing future acquisition, not\\nmerely exposing today''s directory as static files.\\n\\nApplication-controlled freshness and local ETag validation provide browser\\nreuse without importing ArenaNet''s cache policy. Finite freshness was chosen\\nover immutable caching to allow repair of corrupt or incorrectly migrated\\nlocal content to become visible after revalidation. Disk retention is a\\nseparate policy and does not expire at the browser freshness boundary.\\n\\nCosts are real: safe fetching, bounded misses, atomic persistence, failure\\nhandling and persistent storage now belong to the backend. The Product Owner\\nexplicitly requested that ownership. Coalescing and resource bounds keep\\nhundreds of visible-item misses from creating unbounded work; no measured speed\\nimprovement or seven-second compliance is claimed. A completely cold\\napplication cache still needs upstream downloads.\\n\\nThis uses existing filesystem/HTTP facilities with no new library selection\\nor associated support lifecycle. Deployment needs a persistent mount in its\\nexisting scope, not an additional service. Changing the storage adapter later\\nis possible behind the stable application field; cache contents are\\nreconstructible, but ordinary restart/redeployment must preserve them.\\n\\n## Alternatives Considered\\n\\n| Option | Benefit for this project | Deciding limitation |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Shared source-versioned filesystem cache (selected) | Persistent reuse, shared desktop/web acquisition, no new service | Backend owns safe misses and storage; legacy migration needs provenance |\\n| Serve legacy item-ID files with a source-version sidecar (runner-up) | Less initial file movement; reuses desktop layout | Requires coordinated image/sidecar updates, stale-version detection and browser versioning; existing files still lack provenance and identical images stay duplicated by item |\\n| Direct ArenaNet URLs with browser caching | Least backend implementation | Fails Request-005 whenever a cold browser needs an already-used application image |\\n| Reverse-proxy cache | Provides HTTP caching and serving | Adds configuration/runtime ownership, still needs canonical metadata checks, persistent storage and desktop reuse; no demonstrated benefit over the existing backend |\\n| Database binaries, distributed cache or object service | Centralized or multi-instance storage | No documented scale/availability need; adds operations and moving parts explicitly disfavored by Request-005 |\\n| Bulk mirror or custom browser persistent store | Prefetch or browser-managed retention | Does not match on-demand scope; browser storage alone cannot meet application-owned persistence across browsers |\\n\\n## Consequences\\n\\nThe previous direct-browser exception is removed. All web item icons go through\\nthe application, and the implementation must prove cached operation during\\nupstream outage and after restart. A new application cache entry is successful\\nonly after persistence. Missing images retain usable placeholder rendering.\\n\\nLegacy files without trustworthy provenance may need one on-demand upstream\\nretrieval before entering the versioned cache; existing files are not deleted\\nor universally declared trustworthy. Future writes converge on the shared\\nstore. Retention consumes disk, with no invented capacity estimate or need\\nfor scheduled eviction. Storage pressure can motivate a later bounded decision.\\n\\nPlanner follow-up: rescope the two blocked icon stories, carry the persistence\\nconstraint into normal deployment planning, retain Phase 5 gates, and assess\\nintegrated delivery/performance before closing Request-005. This ADR resolves\\nthe architecture question only. No User Decision, new story, implementation,\\ntest result or Product Owner request closure is created.\\n\\n## References and evidence\\n\\n- [TARGET_ARCHITECTURE section 12.1](../../TARGET_ARCHITECTURE.md#121-web-presentation-assets-and-product-requirements): normative contract.\\n- [Request-005](../../../agent/product-owner-requests/Request-005-changeArchitect-desicion.md): revised product intent.\\n- [CURRENT_ARCHITECTURE](../../CURRENT_ARCHITECTURE.md), sections 5.4, 5.11 and 5.12; [ROADMAP](../../ROADMAP.md), Phase 5.\\n- Read-only inspection: "'`src/main/java/sync/IconSync.java`, `IconSyncGateway.java`, and `src/main/java/repo/AppConfig.java`.'"\\n- [GW2 render service](https://wiki.guildwars2.com/wiki/API%3ARender_service): signature/file-ID paths and PNG/JPG formats, checked via indexed official wiki on 2026-09-25; direct fetch was unavailable. No live upstream headers or availability verified.\\n- [RFC 9111](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html): HTTP freshness, validators and no-store semantics; [RFC 8246](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8246.html): immutable semantics. Checked 2026-09-25. The selected TTL and disk-retention policy are project engineering choices, not upstream guarantees.\\n\")
